@page "/pv-system"
@attribute [Authorize]
@rendermode InteractiveServer
@inject PvSystemInfo Site
@inject EvInfo Car
@inject ChargingLimits Limits

@*
    The installation, as configured (issue #111). Read-only on purpose and not merely for now: every
    value here is resolved once at startup — the Modbus clients are constructed from it, and the
    resolver has already refused anything that could not be used — so a control that edited it would
    be editing a copy of a decision that has already been made. Changing any of it means editing the
    configuration and restarting, and the page says so rather than leaving someone hunting for a
    Save button.

    What it is for: answering "which system is this, and what is it talking to?" from a browser, the
    question the startup log answers once and then buries.
*@

<SiteTitle Page="PV system" />

<h1>PV system</h1>

<p class="muted">
    Read from the configuration at startup. Changing any of it means editing <code>.env</code> or
    <code>appsettings.json</code> and restarting the controller.
</p>

<h2>Identity</h2>

<dl class="facts">
    <dt>Name</dt>
    <dd>@Text(Site.Name)</dd>

    <dt>Id</dt>
    <dd>@Text(Site.Id)</dd>

    <dt>Address</dt>
    <dd>@Text(Site.Address)</dd>
</dl>

@if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Site.Id))
{
    <p class="table-note muted">
        This system has no id yet. It is optional today; it becomes the name the controller publishes
        under, so that two installations can share one MQTT broker. Set <code>Pv__Id</code> and
        <code>Pv__Name</code> to claim one.
    </p>
}

<h2>Location and orientation</h2>

<dl class="facts">
    <dt>Coordinates</dt>
    <dd>
        @if (Site.HasLocation)
        {
            @($"{Site.Latitude!.Value.ToString("0.####", Invariant)}, {Site.Longitude!.Value.ToString("0.####", Invariant)}")
        }
        else
        {
            <span class="muted">—</span>
        }
    </dd>

    <dt>Azimuth</dt>
    <dd>@Azimuth()</dd>

    <dt>Tilt</dt>
    <dd>@Degrees(Site.TiltDegrees)</dd>
</dl>

@if (!Site.HasLocation)
{
    <p class="table-note muted">
        Without coordinates no weather is recorded against a charging session — the feature is off
        rather than wrong. Set <code>Pv__Latitude</code> and <code>Pv__Longitude</code>.
    </p>
}

<h2>Array</h2>

<dl class="facts">
    <dt>Peak DC capacity</dt>
    <dd>@Number(Site.CapacityKwp, "kWp")</dd>

    <dt>Inverter capacity</dt>
    <dd>@Number(Site.InverterCapacityKw, "kW")</dd>

    <dt>Loss factor</dt>
    <dd>@Number(Site.LossFactor, format: "0.00")</dd>

    <dt>Commissioned</dt>
    <dd>@(Site.InstallDate is { } date ? date.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd") : Missing)</dd>
</dl>

<h2>Devices</h2>

<table class="sessions">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Role</th>
            <th>Name</th>
            <th>Model</th>
            <th>Address</th>
            <th>Unit</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>Inverter</td>
            <td>@Text(Site.Inverter.Name)</td>
            <td>@Text(Site.Inverter.Model)</td>
            <td>@Address(Site.Inverter)</td>
            <td>@Site.Inverter.Connection.UnitId</td>
        </tr>
        @foreach (var charger in Site.Chargers)
        {
            <tr>
                <td>Charger <span class="muted">@charger.Id</span></td>
                <td>@Text(charger.Name)</td>
                <td>@Text(charger.Model)</td>
                <td>@Address(charger)</td>
                <td>@charger.Connection.UnitId</td>
            </tr>
        }
    </tbody>
</table>

<p class="table-note muted">
    A model is what the box is, not what the controller speaks: the register maps are compiled for the
    devices in the README's hardware targets and are chosen by nothing here. One charger is supported;
    the configuration is a list so that it need not change shape the day a second one can be driven.
</p>

<h2>The car</h2>

@if (!Car.IsConfigured)
{
    <p class="muted" id="no-vehicle">
        No car is described, so every charging limit is the installation's own and no target can be
        asked for as a state of charge. Describe one under <code>Ev:Vehicles:0</code> to say what its
        pack holds and what currents it will actually accept.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <dl class="facts">
        <dt>Name</dt>
        <dd>@Text(Car.Name)</dd>

        <dt>Id</dt>
        <dd>@Text(Car.Id)</dd>

        <dt>Model</dt>
        <dd>@Text(string.Join(" ", new[] { Car.Make, Car.Model }.Where(part => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(part))))</dd>

        <dt>Usable battery</dt>
        <dd>@(Car.CanTargetSoc ? $"{Car.BatteryCapacityKWh.ToString("0.#", Invariant)} kWh" : Missing)</dd>

        <dt>Charge efficiency</dt>
        <dd>@Car.ChargeEfficiency.ToString("P0", Invariant)</dd>
    </dl>

    <h3>What it will accept</h3>

    @*
        Three columns rather than one, because the interesting number is the third: what the car says,
        what the installation says, and which of them the controller is actually working to. A page
        that showed only the car's figures would leave somebody wondering why a 32 A car charges at 16.
    *@
    <table class="grid">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>&nbsp;</th><th>The car</th><th>The installation</th><th>In effect</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Minimum current</td>
                <td>@Amps(Car.MinChargingCurrentAmps)</td>
                <td>@Amps(Limits.MinAmps == Car.MinChargingCurrentAmps ? null : Limits.MinAmps)</td>
                <td>@Amps(Limits.MinAmps)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Maximum current</td>
                <td>@Amps(Car.MaxChargingCurrentAmps)</td>
                <td>@Amps(Limits.MaxAmps == Car.MaxChargingCurrentAmps ? null : Limits.MaxAmps)</td>
                <td>@Amps(Limits.MaxAmps)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Phases</td>
                <td>@Count(Car.Phases)</td>
                <td>@Count(Limits.Phases == Car.Phases ? null : Limits.Phases)</td>
                <td>@Count(Limits.Phases)</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="table-note muted">
        The controller works to the narrower of the two. A car can only ever <em>lower</em> a limit —
        the installation's maximum is the site's supply and the wiring in the wall, not a preference —
        and anything the car leaves unstated is simply the installation's figure. Phases matter more
        than they look: every power figure the controller reasons with, including when a deferred fast
        charge has to start, is computed from the count in the last column.
    </p>
}

@code {
    private const string Missing = "—";

    private static readonly System.Globalization.CultureInfo Invariant =
        System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture;

    // The eight points are enough to catch the mistake this is here to catch -- an array described as
    // facing north when it faces south -- and more would suggest a precision the bearing does not have.
    private static readonly string[] Compass =
        ["north", "north-east", "east", "south-east", "south", "south-west", "west", "north-west"];

    private static string Text(string value) => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value) ? Missing : value;

    private static string Amps(int? value) => value is { } amps ? $"{amps} A" : Missing;

    private static string Count(int? value) => value is { } number ? number.ToString(Invariant) : Missing;

    private static string Address(PvDeviceInfo device) => $"{device.Connection.Host}:{device.Connection.Port}";

    private static string Degrees(double? value) =>
        value is { } number ? $"{number.ToString("0.#", Invariant)}°" : Missing;

    private static string Number(double? value, string? unit = null, string format = "0.##")
    {
        if (value is not { } number)
        {
            return Missing;
        }

        var text = number.ToString(format, Invariant);
        return unit is null ? text : $"{text} {unit}";
    }

    // The bearing with the direction it means spelled out, because "172°" is only checkable by
    // someone who already knows which way their roof faces in degrees.
    private string Azimuth()
    {
        if (Site.AzimuthDegrees is not { } bearing)
        {
            return Missing;
        }

        var point = Compass[(int)Math.Round(bearing / 45) % Compass.Length];
        return $"{Degrees(bearing)} ({point})";
    }
}
